WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.277
A transfer request mostly waits.

2
00:00:05.277 --> 00:00:11.076
At 2,000 requests a second and 150 milliseconds each, 300 are in flight.

3
00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:15.040
If the fraud service slows to 2 seconds, 4,000 are.

4
00:00:15.267 --> 00:00:18.616
Virtual threads make that many waiting threads cheap.

5
00:00:18.616 --> 00:00:22.661
But unlimited threads mean unlimited calls to the fraud service.

6
00:00:23.367 --> 00:00:24.724
Follow one request.

7
00:00:24.724 --> 00:00:31.295
It gets its own virtual thread, and one deadline: 300 milliseconds for all of its screening.

8
00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:34.869
It runs the fraud and limits checks at the same time.

9
00:00:34.869 --> 00:00:38.873
Fraud is slow and shared, so each call needs one of 20 permits.

10
00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:42.345
On a normal day, fraud answers in 20 milliseconds.

11
00:00:42.345 --> 00:00:47.796
The permit goes back, both checks pass, and the transfer goes on to the ledger pool.

12
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:53.404
That pool is a limit too: 8 threads and 80 slots, and a 503 when it's full.

13
00:00:53.933 --> 00:00:54.993
Now an outage.

14
00:00:54.993 --> 00:00:57.492
Every fraud call takes 2 seconds.

15
00:00:57.492 --> 00:00:59.991
Twenty calls hold all 20 permits.

16
00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:02.408
A new request arrives, for 40.00.

17
00:01:02.408 --> 00:01:04.013
Its limits check passes.

18
00:01:04.013 --> 00:01:09.031
Its fraud call waits for a permit, but only as long as its deadline allows.

19
00:01:09.233 --> 00:01:11.403
Another request runs out of time.

20
00:01:11.403 --> 00:01:17.058
It interrupts its own fraud call, and that call's finally block hands the permit back.

21
00:01:17.058 --> 00:01:17.979
Ours takes it.

22
00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:19.937
Our clock keeps running.

23
00:01:19.937 --> 00:01:21.456
What happens at zero?

24
00:01:22.867 --> 00:01:23.860
The same thing.

25
00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:27.104
It interrupts its call, and the permit goes back.

26
00:01:27.333 --> 00:01:29.359
Then the stand-in rule decides.

27
00:01:29.359 --> 00:01:34.195
Up to 50.00 goes through without a fraud answer, so our 40.00 is accepted.

28
00:01:34.195 --> 00:01:36.548
Larger amounts get a fast try again.

29
00:01:37.167 --> 00:01:46.859
In the lesson's demo, during the outage, every reply came within about 320 milliseconds, and fraud never saw more than 20 calls at once.

30
00:01:48.267 --> 00:01:53.941
In code, the deadline is computed once, and the permit wait uses what is left of it.

31
00:01:53.941 --> 00:01:55.427
Java and Kotlin match.

32
00:01:57.233 --> 00:01:57.889
The rule.

33
00:01:57.889 --> 00:02:08.383
Threads are free, so every limit sits on a resource: 20 permits for fraud, 8 threads and 80 slots for the journal, and one deadline per request.

34
00:02:08.600 --> 00:02:09.573
Check yourself.

35
00:02:09.573 --> 00:02:11.842
Drop the interrupt at the deadline.

36
00:02:11.842 --> 00:02:15.084
During the outage, what happens to the 20 permits?

37
00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:25.551
Orphaned fraud calls keep them for the full 2 seconds, so new requests get no permit, even for a call that would succeed.
